BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lawai-academic-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T185149Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Law & AI Academic Fellowship
DESCRIPTION:The Institute for Law & AI (LawAI)\, an independent think tank 
 researching and advising on the legal challenges posed by artificial intel
 ligence\, is running its inaugural Law & AI Academic Fellowship for aspiri
 ng legal scholars who intend to go on the US legal academic job market. Th
 e fellowship is designed as an alternative to university-sponsored Visitin
 g Assistant Professor or law fellow positions: fellows spend the majority 
 of their time researching and writing articles for publication in law jour
 nals on the law of transformative AI\, and are prepared for the academic j
 ob market as part of a larger application package. Teaching is not require
 d\, which LawAI frames as a benefit over most university-affiliated positi
 ons\, though fellows who want teaching experience are encouraged to seek i
 t and LawAI and its affiliates may be able to help secure positions. Each 
 fellow is paired with an academic mentor from LawAI's network of affiliate
 d academics for ongoing support\, with more intensive help in the final st
 ages of the job application process including moot interviews and detailed
  feedback. Fellows may also inquire about spending time in residence at ac
 ademic institutions of interest. Candidates are selected in part on how we
 ll their research interests align with LawAI's: the institute expects AI t
 o reshape culture\, the economy\, public policy\, public administration\, 
 politics\, military affairs and geostrategy at least as profoundly as the 
 Industrial Revolution\, and asks whether a candidate's research is well-sc
 oped to changes of that magnitude. Areas of particular interest include ri
 sk regulation of transformative AI\, liability for AI harms\, AI and biose
 curity\, AI and cybersecurity\, executive branch use of transformative AI\
 , national security law\, law and AI infrastructure\, administrative law\,
  constitutional law\, international regulation\, AI in the judiciary\, and
  industrial policy. Full-time two-year role at USD 130\,000 per year\, bas
 ed in Washington DC\, Cambridge UK\, or remote. Applications close 31 July
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: Institute for Law & AI (LawAI)\nLocation: Washingt
 on DC\, USA\; Cambridge\, UK\; or remote\nAward: USD 130\,000 annual salar
 y. No application fee.\nDuration: Two years\, full-time\n\nMore: https://l
 aw-ai.org/career/academic-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://law-ai.org/career/academic-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington DC\, USA\; Cambridge\, UK\; or remote
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:albee-foundation-barn-residency-winter-spring-2027@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T185149Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260914
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260915
SUMMARY:Deadline: Edward F. Albee Foundation: William Flanagan Memorial Cre
 ative Persons Center Residency (Winter/Spring 2027)
DESCRIPTION:Edward Albee founded this foundation in 1962 with the proceeds 
 from Who's Afraid of Virginia Woolf?\, and it has run The Barn in Montauk 
 for close to sixty years as a place for writers and visual artists to get 
 uninterrupted time. The house sits on a secluded knoll and takes four resi
 dents at a time\, usually two writers and two visual artists\, each with a
 n en-suite bedroom and a separate studio. The environment is deliberately 
 simple and communal: residents share responsibility for the space and are 
 expected to work seriously and keep it quiet for everyone else. ELIGIBILIT
 Y: any creative person over 18\, any nationality\, students included\; sel
 ection uses talent and need as the only criteria. There are no fees at all
 \, to apply or to stay. Domestic Fellows get a USD 2\,500 stipend on compl
 eting the residency\; international Fellows get expenses reimbursed up to 
 USD 2\,500. DATES: this cycle covers residencies from January to June 2027
 . Applications open at 08:00 on 17 August 2026 and close on 14 September 2
 026\, through apply.albeefoundation.org. The next cycle (July-November 202
 7) opens 11 January 2027 and closes 15 February 2027. The Barn is closed e
 very December. Contact apply@albeefoundation.org or (212) 226-2020.\n\nOrg
 anisation: The Edward F. Albee Foundation\nLocation: Montauk\, Long Island
 \, New York\, USA (about two miles from downtown Montauk and the Atlantic)
 \nAward: Fully funded residency: private en-suite bedroom and separate wor
 king studio\, USD 2\,500 stipend for domestic Fellows paid on successful c
 ompletion\, or reimbursement of expenses capped at USD 2\,500 for internat
 ional Fellows. No application fee and no residency fee.\nDuration: One ful
 l calendar month between January and June 2027\; partial months are not of
 fered\n\nMore: https://apply.albeefoundation.org/?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://apply.albeefoundation.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Montauk\, Long Island\, New York\, USA (about two miles from downt
 own Montauk and the Atlantic)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:south-arts-southern-prize-literature-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T185149Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261007
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261008
SUMMARY:Deadline: Southern Prize for Literature 2027
DESCRIPTION:South Arts' Southern Prize for Literature advances the work of 
 writers engaged in book-length manuscript development\, with an unrestrict
 ed top award of USD 25\,000 and two runner-up awards of USD 10\,000. Becau
 se the money is unrestricted\, it is explicitly intended to buy the thing 
 writers most lack: time. South Arts names time replacement\, research and 
 travel\, workspace\, equipment\, and professional services such as editing
  and manuscript consultation as the kinds of costs it is meant to cover. E
 LIGIBILITY: individual writers aged 18 or over who are full-time residents
  of the nine-state South Arts region (Alabama\, Florida\, Georgia\, Kentuc
 ky\, Louisiana\, Mississippi\, North Carolina\, South Carolina and Tenness
 ee)\, who are not enrolled in a degree programme\, and who are working as 
 sole author on an original literary manuscript. Applications opened 12 Aug
 ust 2026\; award notifications in November 2026\, with a final report due 
 30 June 2027. NOTE: this call carries a USD 25 application fee. Deadline 7
  October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: South Arts\nLocation: USA. Open to full-ti
 me residents of the nine-state South Arts region: Alabama\, Florida\, Geor
 gia\, Kentucky\, Louisiana\, Mississippi\, North Carolina\, South Carolina
  and Tennessee.\nAward: One winner receives USD 25\,000 and two runners-up
  receive USD 10\,000 each. The award is unrestricted and is framed as supp
 orting time replacement\, research and travel\, workspace\, equipment\, an
 d professional services such as editing and manuscript consultation. APPLI
 CATION FEE: USD 25 at submission.\nDuration: Unrestricted award supporting
  book-length manuscript development\n\nMore: https://www.southarts.org/gra
 nts-opportunities/southern-prize-literature?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.southarts.org/grants-opportunities/southern-prize-literatur
 e?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:USA. Open to full-time residents of the nine-state South Arts regi
 on: Alabama\, Florida\, Georgia\, Kentucky\, Louisiana\, Mississippi\, Nor
 th Carolina\, South Carolina and Tennessee.
END:VEVENT
